#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The check of leanexe across machines: one build, then the items of tests/items.py at once.

tools/check runs on the primary machine of a hosts file, whose tree it builds with one `lake build`
of every target that the chosen items need, with as many Lake jobs as the machine's budget holds at
7 GiB a job and as tools/leanrun's threads allow, since each job runs as many threads as there are
jobs.  It copies the built tree with rsync over `tailscale ssh` to each other machine at the
same path, and then runs the items, each where its tools are present, as many at once as each
machine's budget allows.  A machine's budget is its memory and CPUs less a reserve, less the
reserved work that the hosts file names, and less the limits of the other systemd scopes and
services running there, read every few seconds: MemoryMax and CPUQuota, or the memory in use where
a scope has no limit.  An item starts only when its memory and CPUs fit what the items running
there leave of the budget, when the memory available leaves half the reserve after the item and the
running items reach their memory, and when the load is below the CPUs.  An item counts its declared
CPUs, its scope's quota, which the manifest sets from the use that build/check/cpus.json records,
since items counted below their quotas reach them together.  Each item runs in a systemd
user scope with its memory as MemoryMax and no swap, and its Lean commands run in the scopes of
tools/leanrun, with a lock directory of their own.

An item whose dependency failed or did not run does not run, and a failure stops no other item.  The
outputs of an item that another machine's item reads go to that machine through the primary.  An
item whose inputs hash to the key of its recorded pass is not run again, unless --all is given, and
the status counts its recorded log.  --affected selects the items of the drivers, modules, and Lean
files that tools/affected reports for a change, with their dependencies.

The run writes build/check/logs/ITEM.log, build/check/results.json, build/check/items.md with each
item's machine, time, and memory, build/check/samples-HOST.txt with the readings of each machine,
and STATUS.md, the steps of tools/full-check with their statuses, times, and counts, which the
script also copies to the root after the checks of tools/full-check on its text.  It exits with
status 1 when an item failed or did not run.

The hosts file is JSON:
  {"root": PATH, "hosts": [{"name": NAME, "primary": true, "items": true,
    "rank": N, "reserve": {"mem": GIB, "cpus": N}, "limit": {"mem": GIB, "cpus": N},
    "item_max": GIB, "peak_max": GIB,
    "reserved": [{"what": TEXT, "dir": PATH, "mem": GIB, "cpus": N}], "env": {VAR: VALUE}}, ...]}
root is the tree's path on every machine.  One machine is the primary.  items false keeps items off
a machine, and limit caps its budget; item_max bounds the memory of an item there and of each of
its scopes, and peak_max admits only items whose peak, the larger of the manifest's estimate and
the recorded peak, or else the item's memory, lies below it.  The run reads reserve, limit,
reserved, item_max, and peak_max again every ten seconds.  An item that runs on any processor goes
first to the machines with the fewest tools.  Of the others, an item goes to the machine of lowest
rank, 0 unless set, that holds it, and among those to the one with the most memory left.  A
reserved entry stands for other work whose scopes run in dir, so that only the entry counts for
them.  env holds the variables of the machine's tools, such as PATH, CCOMP, CLIGHTGEN,
CCOMP_STDLIB, COMPCERT_TREE, QEMU, and QEMU_USER.

Items that fit no machine's budget while no item runs wait --wait seconds, 120 unless given, for
other work to end, and then do not run.

--report writes items.md and STATUS.md again from the last run's results.json and logs.

Usage: tools/check --hosts FILE [--all | --affected [BASE]] [--only REGEX] [--list] [--no-build]
  [--wait SECONDS] | --report
"""

